Context: the preceding pages (69-73) treated the group H = GP(1,C) ≅ PGL_2(C) acting on P^1 (and the integral form PGL_2 over Z acting on P^1_Z). The differential invariant of that action on holomorphic functions is the Schwarzian-type cocycle c_0(f,g). It takes values in ω^{⊗2}, and the associated torsor 𝒯 (projective structures) is a torsor under the quadratic differentials. Page 73 also asked how to characterize c_0 in characteristic p.

This page opens a short survey of the 'analogous developments for other groups', i.e. the subgroups of the projective group of the line.

(a) Translation group G_a acting on the line E^1. On a 1-dimensional complex manifold X there is the de Rham differential d: O_X → Ω^1_{X/C} = ω_X. It is an epimorphism of sheaves because X is smooth of dimension 1: every 1-form is closed, so the holomorphic Poincaré lemma applies. Its kernel is exactly the locally constant functions. Grothendieck sets A(f,g) = dg − df, a map A: O_X × O_X → ω_X. This A satisfies the analogues of the axioms a), b) used earlier for the Schwarzian cocycle:
- cocycle/additivity: A(f,g) + A(g,h) = A(f,h), and A(f,f) = 0;
- invariance under the group acting on the target: A(f, g+c) = A(f,g) for c ∈ C;
- local rigidity: A(f,g) = A(f,h) together with g(x) = h(x) implies g = h near x;
- surjectivity: for fixed f, the map g ↦ A(f,g) from O_X to ω_X is an epimorphism.

Because of these properties, O_X/C ≅ ω_X canonically. So the torsor which in the PGL_2 case was a nontrivial ω^{⊗2}-torsor is here trivial ('si on veut, il est trivial'), and there is no need to introduce a torsor at all.

The marginal note corrects the setup: the right sheaf is not O_X but E_X, the sheaf of étale maps to E^1, i.e. local coordinates. The next page accordingly replaces ω_X by ω_X^* (nowhere-vanishing forms, an O_X^*-torsor) and dg − df by dg/df ∈ O_X^*. It then treats (b) homotheties (G_m acting on E^1 or E^{1*}): L^*/C^* ≅ Conn(L), via B(f,g) = d(g/f)/(g/f).

The overall picture is the hierarchy G_a ⊂ G_m ⋉ G_a ⊂ PGL_2. Its levels are:
- translation structures: nowhere-vanishing 1-forms;
- homothety and affine structures: connections, a torsor under ω;
- projective structures: a torsor under ω^{⊗2}, governed by the Schwarzian.
Each structure is described by a group-invariant 'differential cocycle' A(f,g) satisfying a short list of axioms.
